  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "Why do solar farm panels tilt?",
      "a": "Panels collect most when sunlight hits them square on. Fixed rows are tilted towards the midday sun, and trackers turn the panels through the day to follow it, collecting about 15 to 25 percent more over a year.",
      "asks": [
        "What is a solar tracker?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What does an inverter do in a solar farm?",
      "a": "It turns the panels' direct current into alternating current matching the grid's voltage and frequency, using fast electronic switches. It also finds the best operating point so the panels give their most power.",
      "asks": [
        "Why does solar power need an inverter?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why do solar farms add batteries?",
      "a": "Solar output peaks at midday, when demand is often lower, and drops to zero at night. Batteries store spare midday energy and release it in the evening peak, making the solar power more useful.",
      "asks": [
        "Can solar farms work at night?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How much land does a solar farm need?",
      "a": "Roughly 1.5 to 2.5 hectares for each megawatt, depending on how the rows are spaced. Sheep can often graze between the rows, and some solar farms float on reservoirs or cover car parks instead.",
      "asks": [
        "How big is a solar farm?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Does a solar farm work on cloudy days?",
      "a": "Yes, but less. Panels still make electricity from light scattered through cloud, often 10 to 25 percent of their sunny output, and nothing at night.",
      "asks": [
        "Do solar panels work when it is cloudy?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "Solar panels make direct current, so solar farms use inverters to turn it into alternating current for the grid.",
    "Single-axis trackers, which turn panels to follow the sun, typically raise a solar farm's yearly output by around 15 to 25 percent."
  ],
